A family's yearly spending on all types of insurance is uniformly distributed from USD 400 to USD 3,800. Find:

a. the mean;

b. the standard deviation;

c. $P(X<2000)$;

d. $P(X>3000)$;

e. $P(1500<X<2500)$.

Weekly incomes of shift foremen are normally distributed with mean USD 1,000 and standard deviation USD 100. Find the probability that a randomly selected foreman's income is:

1. between USD 790 and USD 1,000;

2. less than USD 790.

Home-loan amounts requested are normally distributed with mean USD 70,000 and standard deviation USD 20,000. Find the probability that a randomly selected application requests:

a. at least USD 80,000;

b. between USD 65,000 and USD 80,000;

c. at least USD 65,000.

For a confidence interval for a population mean, state whether the margin of error becomes larger or smaller when:

a. the confidence level increases;

b. the sample size increases;

c. the confidence level decreases;

d. the sample size decreases.
